  by: agent  # f124
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f124
  clock: 1  # f124
  nonce: f124  # f124
  data:  # f124
    kind: invariant  # f124
    title: "Reserved provenance fields exist in the schema but are never populated"  # f124
    status: violated  # f124
    priority: 1  # f124
    parent:  # f124
    tags:  # f124
    - dogfood_corpus_20260528  # f124
    - dogfood_integrated  # f124
    - dogfood_severity_significant  # f124
    isbefore: []  # f124
    duplicate_of: []  # f124
    not_duplicate_of: []  # f124
    pr_ref:  # f124
    description: "Two distinct sets of provenance fields are declared in the schema and serialized in production output but never populated by any writer. One set is on the per-pass record (`AnalysisRun.failed_files`, `AnalysisRun.warnings`) — meant to surface analyzer failures and warnings — uniformly empty across all 84 runs despite known analyzer failures in those same runs. The other set is on the classification provenance record (`limits.supply_chain.classification_failures`, `limits.supply_chain.ambiguous_paths`) — meant to capture classification failures and ambiguities — uniformly empty despite documented mis-classifications the project itself acknowledges. The dataclasses and serialization exist; no writer code populates them.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) `limits.supply_chain.classification_failures = []` and `limits.supply_chain.ambiguous_paths = []` despite tier classifier demonstrably mis-labeling workspace packages.** Multiple known failures should appear in `classification_failures` but don't: workspace packages mis-tiered (144 `hypergumbo_*` modules classified `internal_dep` with `path='<external>'`), 331 first-party symbols emitted as `external_symbol`, workspace `pyproject.toml` files tagged `external_dep`. The classification_failures field is a stub — the tier classifier doesn't write to it when it mis-classifies. A consumer reading `classification_failures=[]` would conclude tier classification succeeded for all paths, but it doesn't.\n\n**(2) `analysis_runs[].failed_files` and `analysis_runs[].warnings` universally empty across all 84 runs.** Known issues NOT reported through these channels: the YAML analyzer processes 174 files producing 0 nodes from 173/174 (no warning); the Solidity `\"Not owner\"` require-msg propagating as a language value (no warning); multiple linkers consuming 1+ seconds with zero productive output (no warning). Per-pass schema fields exist (`failed_files: List[str]`, `warnings: List[str]`) but the channels are unused. Each language analyzer reports its own failures/warnings through stdout/stderr or never reports them at all; the orchestrator never aggregates analyzer warnings into the structured record.\n\n### Invariant\n\nA schema field declared and serialized should be populated when the relevant condition arises. If a feature is acknowledged as planned-but-not-implemented, the field should be omitted at serialization time or the schema should mark it as unimplemented so consumers do not read `[]` as \"no failures occurred.\" Reserved-but-never-populated arrays produce silent false-negative reports.\n\n### Scope\n\nAffects every per-pass record (failure and warning arrays uniformly empty across 84 runs) and the classification provenance record (uniformly empty across all runs). The schema declares these fields as the canonical reporting channel.\n\n### Exposure\n\n`behavior_map.analysis_runs[].failed_files`, `analysis_runs[].warnings`, `limits.supply_chain.classification_failures`, `limits.supply_chain.ambiguous_paths`.\n\n### Consequence\n\nConsumers reading the empty arrays conclude that no failures or misclassifications occurred. The actual failures and misclassifications exist; the consumer is silently misled about pipeline health. A monitoring dashboard or CI gate built against these fields would never fire even when systematic failures are occurring.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Wire each language analyzer to write its failures into `AnalysisRun.failed_files` and its warnings into `AnalysisRun.warnings` at emit time. Adopt a per-analyzer convention; document it in the schema.\n- Wire the tier classifier to write to `limits.supply_chain.classification_failures` whenever a path is mis-classified by its own heuristics; write to `limits.supply_chain.ambiguous_paths` when multiple heuristics produce different answers.\n- Add a property test that asserts at least one of these channels is populated when known-failing analyses are run against a fixture (regression test ensures the channels stay wired).\n- Alternatively, if the channels are intentionally unimplemented, mark them as such in the schema (`status: unimplemented`) so consumers know not to trust them.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-pogif, INV-vukag — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # f124
    fields:  # f124
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-703764"  # f124
      statement: "A schema field declared and serialized should be populated when the relevant condition arises. If a feature is acknowledged as planned-but-not-implemented, the field should be omitted or the schema should mark it as unimplemented so consumers do not read `[]` as \"no failures occurred.\""  # f124
      root_cause: "Two distinct sets of provenance fields are declared in the schema but never populated by production code. One set is on the per-pass provenance record — fields meant to surface analyzer failures and warnings — which are emitted as empty arrays on every record despite known analyzer failures in the same run. The other set is on the classification provenance record — fields meant to capture classification failures and ambiguities — which are emitted as empty arrays despite documented classification cases the project itself acknowledges are misclassified. The dataclasses and serialization exist; no writer code populates them."  # f124
